-# Connection methods
-
-The `method` for `connection` request/store. Pick the most specific
-match: a protocol below when the service authenticates that way, a
-managed provider when it exists, and the generic methods only for what is
-left. Every method except `other` and `native` is brokered: the computer
-holds a placeholder and the broker authenticates requests to the listed
-destinations. A manual that names its own method overrides this list.
-
-- `http` — a key or token on HTTPS requests. Your own field names (match
-  the env variable the tool reads); destinations required.
-- `other` — any other secret (SSH keys, database passwords). Your own
-  fields; no destinations; delivered only when the command names its env
-  variable, or through `credential-run`.
-- `native` — a CLI holding its own login. Store only, no fields.
-- `oauth` — redirect relay for an OAuth app you set up. Request only, no
-  fields; returns a `redirect_uri`.
-- `google-workspace` — managed sign-in owned by the platform. Request
-  only, no fields, no destinations.
-
-## Protocols with fixed fields
-
-`request` takes no `fields`; `store` takes the names below with values.
-Parentheses mark optional fields. Inputs marked * stay in the vault; you
-use the token field's env variable. Destinations required.
-
-| method | fields | token field |
-| --- | --- | --- |
-| `oauth-client-credentials` | `client_id`, `client_secret`*, `token_url`, (`scopes`, `audience`) | `access_token` |
-| `oauth-jwt-bearer` | `private_key`* (PEM), `token_url`, `issuer`, `audience`, (`subject`, `scopes`, `key_id`, `client_id`, `client_secret`*) | `access_token` |
-| `gcp-service-account` | `service_account_key`* (JSON), (`scopes`, `subject`) | `access_token` |
-| `gcp-identity-token` | `service_account_key`*, `audience` | `identity_token` |
-| `aws-sigv4` | `access_key_id`, `secret_access_key`, (`session_token`) | `AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID`, `AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY`, `AWS_SESSION_TOKEN` |
-
-AWS: CLIs and SDKs sign with the placeholders and the broker re-signs;
-large or streamed S3 uploads need `payload_signing_enabled = false` for
-s3 in `~/.aws/config`.
